ly if the encoding changes; the differential suite must stay green throughout.  ## Method  1. Read `AGENTS.md` in full. Obey every hard rule; they are not negotiable. 2. Reproduce the finding. Record the exact command and the exact wrong output. 3. Write the failing test FIRST. Watch it fail. Do not touch production code    until you have a red test that fails for the right reason. 4. Make the smallest change that turns it green. 5. Run the four gates yourself before claiming anything:     ```bash    cargo fmt --all -- --check    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    cargo test --all-targets    ./rbops/verify.sh phase-089    ```  6. Write `REPORT.md` in the exact shape required by `AGENTS.md` section 4.  ## Definition of done  - [ ] `catch error of Io` catches a failed `files.read` and ignores a `1 + "x"` type error in the same body (both directions pinned — a matcher that catches everything proves nothing) - [ ] the untyped `catch error` form behaves byte-identically to before (the existing `catch_binding_test.rs` suite passes unmodified — no silent migration) - [ ] both engines raise the same typed value for the same failure (one differential test per failure kind across tree-walker and bytecode VM, kinds diffed not eyeballed) - [ ] `rb dis` on a program containing a typed catch shows no new operand shape unless the format version bumped, in which case `docs/BYTECODE.md` documents it in the same commit - [ ] SPEC.md documents the type names a program can match (closed list, asserted by a test that matches each one and refuses an invented one by name) - [ ] edge_* tests cover matching a supertype/all-errors form if one exists, a `catch of` with no matching arm falling through to the untyped arm, error values escaping the `catch` body as first-class values (inspectable, printable), and a 200-deep error chain (no stack growth per frame)  ## Test requirements — non-negotiable  From `AGENTS.md` section 3. Tick each row that applies to this change. For every row you do NOT cover, write one line in `REPORT.md` saying why.  - [ ] empty — covered / N/A + why - [ ] singleton — covered / N/A + why - [ ] boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] out_of_bounds — covered / N/A + why - [ ] type_mismatch — covered / N/A + why - [ ] numeric_boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] unicode — covered / N/A + why - [ ] nesting_recursion — covered / N/A + why - [ ] duplicate_missing_keys — covered / N/A + why - [ ] malformed_input — covered / N/A + why - [ ] resource_limit — covered / N/A + why  You must also:  - at least 3 new `#[test]` functions (or at least 2 new Redblue `test` blocks).   This is a floor, not a target: it catches "no verification at all". Whether   your tests COVER the change is judged by the reviewer, and an untested branch   is a MAJOR finding that sends the phase back. - at least 1 test named `edge_*` - at least 1 test that asserts a **failure** is produced, not just a success - zero new `#[ignore]`, `// skip`, or `allow(clippy::` suppressions - zero newly-failing pre-existing tests  ## Constraints  - Do not touch `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/`, or   `rbops/phases.json`. The gate rejects these paths, and the bot token has no   `workflows` scope so a push touching `.github/` is refused by GitHub. - Do not weaken, delete or re-scope any existing test. - Do not change the language surface listed in `phases/INVARIANTS.md`. - Keep the diff on-topic. A refactor smuggled into a bug-fix phase is a   review BLOCKER. - Do not update README or ROADMAP to claim this works. Update them only when   the gate is green and you have run the examples yourself.  ## If you are blocked  Do not fake progress. Write what you tried, the exact error, and what you believe is required into `FINDINGS.md`, then stop.  A blocked phase with an honest report is worth more than a green phase with a lie in it. `verify.sh` will reject the phase anyway; a fabrication only adds a blocking review finding on top.  --- ## CONTINUATION MODE A previous attempt was interrupted and its partial work is already in the tree. DO NOT restart. Inspect what exists, continue from it, finish the phase.
